WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
The first film made haploid cells by separating homologous pairs first, then sister

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.840
chromatids. Why do those cells carry different allele mixes?

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.540
Return to the same model.

4
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.460
Green chromosomes came from one parent, and orange chromosomes came from the other.

5
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.610
Each homolog carries the same genes in matching positions, but its alleles may

6
00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.950
differ. Copying makes sister chromatids.

7
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:34.830
The homologs then lie side by side during the first division, placing matching DNA

8
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.150
regions beside each other.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.620
Crossing over exchanges matching pieces between non-sister chromatids of paired

10
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:48.560
homologs. Watch two chromatids cross, break at matching positions, and reconnect.

11
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.120
Each changed chromatid now carries green and orange sections, combining alleles from

12
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.120
that parent's parents.

13
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.770
A second process mixes whole chromosomes.

14
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:05.050
Each homologous pair can face either direction before it separates.

15
00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.350
One pair's direction does not direct another pair.

16
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.010
This is independent assortment: every pair adds another either-or choice.

17
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:17.673
Look at these paired chromosomes.

18
00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:23.093
The long green homolog and short orange homolog face the same pole.

19
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:28.433
If only the short pair flips, which chromosome mix will enter that side?

20
00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:31.293
Predict before the arrows move.

21
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.600
After the short pair flips, green from both pairs faces the left side.

22
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.860
Separation sends that new whole-chromosome mix left.

23
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.920
Changing one pair changed the outcome, while every final cell still receives a

24
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.920
member of each pair.

25
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:55.543
The count grows by doubling.

26
00:01:56.843 --> 00:01:59.543
For each pair, a gamete can receive either homolog.

27
00:02:00.903 --> 00:02:04.843
Each added pair doubles the running count across many separate meioses.

28
00:02:06.203 --> 00:02:08.263
The screen shows the computed pattern.

29
00:02:09.563 --> 00:02:13.283
Crossing over adds more combinations within those whole chromosomes.

30
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:18.783
The doubling rule counts whole chromosomes and ignores crossing over.

31
00:02:20.043 --> 00:02:21.803
It also has a limit for genes.

32
00:02:23.223 --> 00:02:27.023
Genes close together on one chromosome tend to travel together.

33
00:02:28.403 --> 00:02:31.883
Independent assortment applies to genes on different chromosomes.

34
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:38.757
Meiosis separates homologous pairs first, then sister chromatids, while crossing

35
00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:40.877
over and assortment remix alleles.

36
00:02:42.457 --> 00:02:44.497
Crossing over remixes chromosome sections.

37
00:02:45.797 --> 00:02:47.557
Assortment remixes whole chromosomes.

38
00:02:49.117 --> 00:02:54.577
Fertilization then joins independently made egg and sperm cells, usually giving

39
00:02:54.577 --> 00:02:56.037
siblings different combinations.

40
00:02:57.317 --> 00:03:01.337
The variation is not a promise that every individual survives.

41
00:03:02.717 --> 00:03:05.297
It gives a population more allele combinations.

42
00:03:06.717 --> 00:03:10.637
When conditions change, some combinations may work better than others.

43
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:17.117
Diversity raises the chance that some members survive and reproduce; it does not

44
00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:18.077
guarantee survival.
